 just this morning we got a  groundbreaking report from the same  nikkei asia publication claiming that  the next generation  mack chip has already entered mass  production  this month destined for the redesigned  macbook pros  now i know that the article says the m2  chip  which is causing a bunch of confusion in  the comment section  because all of us are expecting the m1x  chip  not the next generation m2 but if you  look closely at what the article says  they mentioned that it's tentatively  known as the m2  which basically means that the name is  subject to further confirmation  from apple and if you think about it  last year everybody thought that the  first apple silicon chip  was gonna be named the a14x until apple  finally revealed the new m1 chip  for the mac so based on that i still  personally believe  that we're going to be getting the m1x  chip in those redesigned macbook pros  with most likely 12 cores for the cpu  and 16 cores for the gpu  but enough about that let's get back to  the main point of that article  the upcoming m1x chip has already  entered  mass production like right now so  here is the main question that we need  to think about if apple's pumping these  chips out right now  why would they have them sitting around  on shelves for months  until some apple event in the fall  because first of all  we know that these macbook pros are  going to be a huge deal  and they definitely deserve some screen  time at an official apple event  so if they don't show them off at wwdc  the next event would most likely have to  be either in october  or november since september is never  focused on mac products  it's just iphones apple watches and  stuff like that  so why would apple start mass production  of the m1x right now  and have them sitting and waiting around  until october  or november it just doesn't make sense  and that's why i'm calling an  announcement at wwdc  but wait i've got a whole lot more  evidence that points to this  actually happening last year in  september digitimes reported that mass  production for the a14x chip  which ended up being called the m1 would  begin  in the fourth quarter of 2020 which  began  in october and as we know apple  announced those maps on  november 10th so that's a maximum of  five weeks  between starting mass production of the  chip itself  and announcing the actual max so if this  report from nikkei asia is true  and the higher end macbook pro apple  silicon chips have already started  production  right now that gives us around six weeks  between the start of chip production  and apple's wwdc event somewhat matching  up to the m1 chip timeline that we had  last year  and there is one more puzzle piece that  now adds up  and makes total sense apple revealed  their 24-inch redesigned imac  and their new m1 ipad pro at their april  20th  event but they're not even allowing  orders to be made  until the 30th and they said that  shipments won't even happen  until the second half of may and john  prosser recently got info from sources  saying that he believes that the new  ipad pro could launch on may 21st  which is actually over a month after the  april event  so hear me out what if apple announces  the new m1x macbook pros  at wwdc and then does the same exact  thing  pushing the actual launch date an entire  month out  into july this would match up perfectly  to the many reports that claim that the  redesigned macbook pros  are coming in the second half of 2021  which technically starts in  july and if you think about it the first  retina macbook pro was revealed at  apple's wwdc event in 2012  and they also have a history of  releasing macbook pros in july  like when they updated them on july 29th  2014  and then in july of 2018 when they added  six core processors and then once again  in 2019  updating them for the back to school  season so it definitely wouldn't be  surprising to see these redesigned  macbook pros get announced  at wwdc since that would allow apple  